                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Long Gone Days is an old-school, grounded RPG story, with just as much old-school, grounded RPG gameplay. Sadly, that also makes it feel a bit lacking for the modern day. This is perfectly shown by the game's archaic resolution limit of 1080p. The whole game really feels like it isunapologetically about going back to an older way of playing video games.\r\n\r\nThere is very little tutorial, although to be fair, the game doesn't necessarily need a lot of it. The way its systems work can definitely feel quite old, though. Health and action points don't regenerate at save points, and instead have to be recovered through items, defeated enemies, or the environment. It's a very old-school approach that can take some getting used to.\r\n\r\nThe turn-based combat system itself is very standard, but it does its job. There is a good, moderate challenge, with managing resources like health, items, and action points being a big part of the game. That said, I would say the game is overall pretty light on combat. It's often spaced out, with sections of more and less combat, which gives you a bit of time to breathe and recover your resources.\r\n\r\nAll of this points towards the biggest strength of the game: its really grounded feel. This shines especially through the story. Without spoiling anything: you play as Rourke, a soldier in a cult general's private army sent on a mission for power. The game tackles this maturely and unflinchingly, nationalism, militarism, radicalization. Raising uncomfortable questions and, more importantly, refusing easy answers. It commits to the unease.\r\n\r\nOverall, Long Gone Days is a game I would recommend for its story and its human element. It is really a game that tries to go for uncomfortable conclusions and actually deals with them in a mature and grounded fashion. The old-school RPG gameplay may feel dated in places, but alongside the story it creates an experience that I enjoyed for what it is.",
